================================================================================
WHAT THIS PORTAL IS
================================================================================

A staging area for game assets. You upload work, it is checked and reviewed,
and approved work goes into the game's asset store.

It is not a file store you can lean on. Your workspace is meant to hold what
you are working on now, not an archive of everything you have ever made.


================================================================================
GETTING IN
================================================================================

Someone with admin rights invites you by email address. You receive a link:

    /register?token=...

Open it and choose a username and password. The role you were invited as is
fixed by the invitation — you cannot choose it.

The invitation link is valid for 28 days. If it expires you can request a new
one from the same page, up to six times before an administrator has to issue a
fresh invitation.

NOT BUILT: the invitation is not emailed yet. Whoever invites you currently has
to pass the link on themselves.

If your link has been withdrawn, or already used, the page will say so rather
than showing you a form that cannot work.


================================================================================
YOUR WORKSPACE
================================================================================

Private to you. Nobody else can see inside it — not other developers, and not
administrators. If an administrator needs to look, they do it by impersonating
you, which is marked and audited, and is not routine.

Your workspace has a SIZE LIMIT. It is a staging area, so it is not meant to
hold everything. Content that has been approved into the game store does not
count against it — pulling your own approved work back to iterate on it is
free.

The panel shows how much you are using.


================================================================================
UPLOADING
================================================================================

Upload a FOLDER. Its name becomes the asset name:

    bigTree_v04/          ->  asset "bigTree"
      scripts/nuke.nk
      models/tree.blend

The `_v04` is YOUR labelling, your own iteration marker — not the system's
version. Rename the folder as you iterate; `bigTree_v05` is still the same
asset.

The system keeps its own version counter per asset — upload 1, upload 2 — which
is bookkeeping. It is shown under additional details.

The panel tells you what an upload will do BEFORE you do it: which asset it
will join, and which upload number it will become.

WHAT CANNOT BE UPLOADED

  * A folder whose name ends in a file extension. `nuke_v10.nk` is a file, not
    an asset, and is refused.
  * Loose files with no folder — there is no name to take. Pick the asset they
    belong to instead, or name a new one.
  * Anything that would take you over the workspace limit.

NAMES THAT LOOK LIKE AN EXISTING ASSET

If you upload `Assets_v04` and you already have `Asset`, you are asked which
you meant, because a forgotten capital or underscore is usually a typo rather
than a new asset. You can still choose "new asset" — it is a sandbox, and a
badly named asset is yours to live with.


================================================================================
WHAT HAPPENS TO A VERSION
================================================================================

1. TECHNICAL CHECK (npm: "technical validation")
   Automated. It confirms the files are present, the stored content matches
   its recorded size, and the logical paths are unique. If it fails it tells
   you which check failed and why. You can run it as often as you like.

2. SUBMIT FOR CREATIVE APPROVAL
   Only possible once the technical check has passed. This locks the version —
   and the asset — so what a reviewer looks at cannot change underneath them.
   While it is pending you cannot change or delete the asset.

3. REVIEW
   An admin or superuser approves or rejects it. A rejection carries a reason.
   If it is rejected the lock is released and you can work on it again.

4. APPROVED
   Your version is copied into the game's asset store, sharing the same
   underlying files rather than duplicating them. The workspace version stays
   locked, and is marked as being in the game store.

NOT BUILT: a reviewer currently cannot fetch the bytes of a submission, only
its file list. Reviewing is effectively blind until the download endpoint
lands.


================================================================================
DELETING
================================================================================

You can delete anything in your own workspace — a version, or a whole asset.

It is a soft delete. The content stays if anything else uses it (the game
store, or another workspace) and your review history is kept. Only your copy
and your quota go.

You cannot delete something that is under review.

You CAN delete a version that has been approved into the game store: the store
keeps its own copy, so only yours goes.


================================================================================
GETTING YOUR WORK BACK
================================================================================

You can download the files of your own versions. A manifest lists every file
with the path it belongs at and its content hash, so a tool could sync only
what has changed.

NOT BUILT: nothing consumes the manifest yet, so downloading is manual, and
there is no archive download.

NOT BUILT: you cannot yet browse or download the GAME STORE — so there is no
way to pick up your own approved work as the basis for continuing. That is
deliberately deferred until the game itself is being built.


================================================================================
WHAT DOES NOT EXIST YET
================================================================================

